A16_SMSS_72_M016936_044                                                       
                                                                              
Detail of Apollo 16 Soil Mechanics Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer          
(LSRP) Page D-3                                                               
                                                                              
Pre-Flight Load Calibration Table                                             
                                                                              
The upper part of this table is very difficult to read due to poor            
quality of the original paper copy and/or microfilming.  To improve the       
quality, NASA National Space Science Data Center (NSSDC) personnel            
rewrote the upper part of the table, lines 1-23, using the original           
microfilm and the known relationships of the values.  The original,           
entire table is included in this archive as A16_SMSS_72_M016936_043.          
                                                                              
This handwritten table gives the post-flight load calibration data for the    
Apollo 16 Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer (LSRP) flight unit, lunar drum    
S/N 2003.  The tests were done on 19 April 1972. This part of the table       
gives the load calibration.  The first column gives the index number          
corresponding to the initial stting on the LSRP.  The second column,          
"REF.", gives the initial angle  (degrees, minutes, seconds) read on the      
drum.  The following columns are for different loads put on the LSRP.  The    
entries in the columns give the reading on the drum (azimuth) and the         
difference between that and the original reading.  These numbers were used    
to create the preflight load calibration table A16_SMSS_72_M016936_041,       
page D-1.                                                                     
                                                                              
